<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shiga Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shiga Prefecture is a landlocked prefecture of the Kansai region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a>, the country's largest lake, with its capital at Ōtsu. It corresponds to the old province of Ōmi, which lay on the main routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> to eastern Japan and contained castle towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikone">Hikone</a> and Azuchi. It was formed from the former domains in the early 1870s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture.jpg" alt="Shiga Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mangetsuji_Otsu_Shiga02n4500.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shiga Prefecture borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui-prefecture">Fukui Prefecture</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu">Gifu</a> to the northeast. It also borders Mie to the southeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> to the west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s largest lake, lies at its centre and occupies one-sixth of its area. The Seta River is the only natural river that flows out of the lake, running through Kyoto to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-bay">Osaka Bay</a>. The lake divides the prefecture into four areas: Kohoku to the north, Kosei to the west, Kotō to the east and Konan to the south, and mountain ranges enclose it, among them the Hira Mountains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>, the Ibuki Mountains and the Suzuka Mountains. Southern Shiga is usually warm, while the north is cold with high snowfall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Shiga was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>, or Gōshū, before the prefectural system was established. Ōmi neighboured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, at the junction of western and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Between 667 and 672 Emperor Tenji founded a palace at Ōtsu, and in 742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> founded one at Shigaraki. In the early Heian period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a>, born in the north of Ōtsu, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a>, the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a>. Oda Nobunaga subjugated Ōmi and built Azuchi Castle on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a> in 1579.</p><p>In 1600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawayama-castle">Sawayama Castle</a>, made war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>. Afterwards Ieyasu made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a> lord of Sawayama, and Naomasa established the Hikone Domain. Ii Naosuke, of that house, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>'s Tairō and concluded commercial treaties with the Western powers. With the abolition of the han system eight prefectures were formed in Ōmi, and they were unified into Shiga Prefecture in September 1872. It is named after Shiga District, to which Ōtsu belonged until 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ōtsu is the capital and largest city; other major cities include Kusatsu, Nagahama and Higashiōmi. Hikone Castle is one of four national treasure castles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The southern half adjoins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and forms part of Greater Kyoto. The Hikiyama festival of floats, held in ten areas including Nagahama each April, was designated an Important Intangible Cultural Property in 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shiga Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After abolition in 1871 it became part of <b>Shiga Prefecture</b>; Hikone castle is a national treasure.</blockquote><a href="/state/hikone-domain">Hikone Domain</a></li></ul></section>
